From: CJ Damsleth <kim@damsleth.no>
Subject: [PATCH] nvme-apple: force a clean ANS boot when a boot stage left it running

apple_nvme_reset_work() assumes a running ANS coprocessor was suspended
by this driver and resumes it with apple_rtkit_wake(). That holds on
machines whose boot stage (m1n1) initializes NVMe and shuts it down
before handoff, but not when the loader never touches NVMe: iBoot's
ANS is still live with iBoot's endpoint and shared-memory state, the
wake handshake adopts none of it, and the ANS firmware crashes
("RTKit crashed; unable to recover without a reboot", observed on a
T6040 J614s with fw 26.5: identify and namespace enumeration complete,
then every read fails and reset reports -5; m1n1's nvme_shutdown() is
guarded by nvme_initialized, so a loader without NVMe support leaves
the coprocessor running).

If the coprocessor reports RUN while our RTKit instance has never
booted it, the state cannot be one we suspended: stop the CPU and fall
into the existing assert/reinit/deassert/boot path instead of waking.

Signed-off-by: CJ Damsleth <kim@damsleth.no>
---
 drivers/nvme/host/apple.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/nvme/host/apple.c b/drivers/nvme/host/apple.c
index 878220681216..08102a0f1c4e 100644
--- a/drivers/nvme/host/apple.c
+++ b/drivers/nvme/host/apple.c
@@ -1098,6 +1098,19 @@ static void apple_nvme_reset_work(struct work_struct *work)
 		writel(0, anv->mmio_coproc + APPLE_ANS_COPROC_CPU_CONTROL);
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * A running coprocessor that this RTKit instance never booted was
+	 * left live by an earlier boot stage (e.g. iBoot through a loader
+	 * without NVMe support). Its endpoint/shared-memory state is not
+	 * ours to wake into; force a stop so the clean boot path below runs.
+	 */
+	if (!apple_rtkit_is_running(anv->rtk) &&
+	    (readl(anv->mmio_coproc + APPLE_ANS_COPROC_CPU_CONTROL) &
+	     APPLE_ANS_COPROC_CPU_CONTROL_RUN)) {
+		dev_warn(anv->dev, "ANS left running by boot stage; forcing clean reset\n");
+		writel(0, anv->mmio_coproc + APPLE_ANS_COPROC_CPU_CONTROL);
+	}
+
 	/*
 	 * Only do the soft-reset if the CPU is not running, which means either we
 	 * or the previous stage shut it down cleanly.
